"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[17364],{117364:e=>{e.exports="# SQL Dialect Conversion — SELECT, DML, Window Functions\n\nRedshift SELECT, DML, CTE, and window-function patterns → DBSQL.\n\n## REDSHIFT\n\n### SELECT patterns\n\n| Redshift                                  | DBSQL                                     | Notes                                                                      |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SELECT TOP 10 *`                         | `SELECT * ... LIMIT 10`                   | Redshift accepts both `TOP N` and `LIMIT N`; DBSQL supports `LIMIT N` only |\n| `LIMIT N`                                 | `LIMIT N`                                 | Identical                                                                  |\n| `LIMIT N OFFSET M`                        | `LIMIT N OFFSET M`                        | Identical                                                                  |\n| `QUALIFY <window-predicate>`              | `QUALIFY <window-predicate>`              | Native in DBSQL — carry through unchanged                                  |\n| `PIVOT (agg(...) FOR col IN ('Q1','Q2'))` | `PIVOT (agg(...) FOR col IN ('Q1','Q2'))` | Same syntax                                                                |\n| `UNPIVOT`                                 | `UNPIVOT`                                 | Supported in DBSQL                                                         |\n\n### LISTAGG — preserve WITHIN GROUP\n\nBoth dialects support `LISTAGG`. Without `WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)`, concatenation order is **not guaranteed** in DBSQL — carry the clause through verbatim even when the source looks redundant.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift / DBSQL (identical)\nSELECT dept, LISTAGG(name, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY hire_date) AS names\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY dept;\n```\n\nRedshift also allows `LISTAGG(DISTINCT col, sep)` — identical in DBSQL.\n\n### Recursive CTEs\n\nRedshift recursive CTEs (`WITH RECURSIVE cte AS (... UNION ALL SELECT ... FROM cte)`) translate directly to DBSQL. Two differences to watch:\n\n- Redshift: `RECURSIVE` keyword is optional (inherited from PostgreSQL).\n- DBSQL: `RECURSIVE` keyword is **required**, and recursive CTEs are supported only on **DBR 17.0+**. On earlier DBR versions, rewrite with iterative scripting or pre-computed depth.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift (RECURSIVE is optional)\nWITH RECURSIVE org (id, parent_id, lvl) AS (\n  SELECT id, parent_id, 0 FROM employees WHERE parent_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT e.id, e.parent_id, o.lvl + 1\n  FROM employees e JOIN org o ON e.parent_id = o.id\n)\nSELECT * FROM org;\n\n-- DBSQL (RECURSIVE is required; add UC three-part names)\nWITH RECURSIVE org (id, parent_id, lvl) AS (\n  SELECT id, parent_id, 0 FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees WHERE parent_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT e.id, e.parent_id, o.lvl + 1\n  FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees e JOIN org o ON e.parent_id = o.id\n)\nSELECT * FROM org;\n```\n\n> For DBSQL recursive CTE depth limits and termination rules, load `skills/writing-sql/recursive-ctes.md`.\n\n### Cursors (anti-pattern)\n\n**Cursors are an anti-pattern in Databricks.** Redshift stored procedures frequently use PL/pgSQL cursors (`FOR row IN SELECT ... LOOP ... END LOOP`). Prefer a set-based rewrite:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift (row-by-row cursor loop in plpgsql)\nFOR rec IN SELECT id FROM source_table LOOP\n    UPDATE other_table SET processed = 1 WHERE id = rec.id;\nEND LOOP;\n\n-- DBSQL (set-based)\nUPDATE other_table\nSET processed = 1\nWHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM source_table);\n```\n\n> For literal cursor translation when row-by-row semantics are strictly required, see `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/redshift/04-plpgsql.md`. Cursor syntax is supported on DBSQL warehouses (DBR 18.1+).\n\n### String concatenation\n\n| Redshift                   | DBSQL                                    | Notes                                                                          |\n| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `str1 \\|\\| str2`           | `str1 \\|\\| str2` or `CONCAT(str1, str2)` | `\\|\\|` is native in DBSQL; both propagate NULL identically — safe substitution |\n| `CONCAT(str1, str2, ...)`  | `CONCAT(str1, str2, ...)`                | Both dialects propagate NULL in multi-arg `CONCAT`                             |\n| `LEN(str)` / `LENGTH(str)` | `LEN(str)` / `LENGTH(str)`               | Both work in DBSQL                                                             |\n\n### JSON / semi-structured extraction\n\nRedshift uses PostgreSQL-style `JSON_EXTRACT_PATH_TEXT` and SUPER-column path operators. DBSQL uses `:` path syntax on VARIANT/STRING or `get_json_object`:\n\n| Redshift                                 | DBSQL                                                                                  | Notes                                                                                                                                                         |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `JSON_EXTRACT_PATH_TEXT(j, 'a', 'b')`    | `j:a.b::STRING` (if column is VARIANT/STRING) or `GET_JSON_OBJECT(j, '$.a.b')`         | DBSQL `:` path traverses VARIANT or parses JSON strings; cast the result as needed. `GET_JSON_OBJECT` is the portable form when the column is typed `STRING`. |\n| `JSON_EXTRACT_ARRAY_ELEMENT_TEXT(j, 0)`  | `j[0]::STRING` (VARIANT) or `GET_JSON_OBJECT(j, '$[0]')`                               |                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `JSON_ARRAY_LENGTH(j)`                   | `CARDINALITY(FROM_JSON(j, 'ARRAY<VARIANT>'))` or `SIZE(FROM_JSON(j, 'ARRAY<STRING>'))` | Pick the inferred element type                                                                                                                                |\n| `IS_VALID_JSON(j)` / `CAN_JSON_PARSE(s)` | `TRY_PARSE_JSON(j) IS NOT NULL`                                                        | Returns **BOOLEAN**. Do not wrap `PARSE_JSON` inside the result — that changes the return type to VARIANT.                                                    |\n| SUPER column `s.a.b`                     | `s:a.b` (VARIANT) or deserialize via `FROM_JSON`                                       | SUPER → VARIANT is the idiomatic migration                                                                                                                    |\n| `JSON_PARSE(str)` / `PARSE_JSON(str)`    | `PARSE_JSON(str)`                                                                      | Returns VARIANT in DBSQL                                                                                                                                      |\n| `JSON_SERIALIZE(super_val)`              | `TO_JSON(variant_val)` or `CAST(variant_val AS STRING)`                                | Serialize VARIANT to JSON string                                                                                                                              |\n| `JSON_SERIALIZE_TO_VARBYTE(super_val)`   | `CAST(TO_JSON(variant_val) AS BINARY)`                                                 | Binary form (rare; prefer string)                                                                                                                             |\n\n### SUPER type-check predicates\n\nRedshift `IS_*` predicates and `JSON_TYPEOF` introspect a SUPER value's content type. DBSQL has no direct equivalents — use `schema_of_variant(v)`, which returns the **uppercase SQL type string** (e.g. `'BIGINT'`, `'ARRAY<...>'`, `'OBJECT<...>'`, `'DECIMAL(p,s)'`). Note: DBSQL's `typeof()` returns the wrapper (`'variant'`), not the inner type — use `schema_of_variant` instead.\n\n| Redshift                       | DBSQL                                                                                             | Notes                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `IS_ARRAY(s)`                  | `STARTS_WITH(schema_of_variant(v), 'ARRAY')`                                                      | `ARRAY<...>` always begins with `ARRAY`                                                                                        |\n| `IS_OBJECT(s)`                 | `STARTS_WITH(schema_of_variant(v), 'OBJECT')`                                                     | `OBJECT<...>` always begins with `OBJECT`                                                                                      |\n| `IS_INTEGER(s)`                | `schema_of_variant(v) IN ('TINYINT', 'SMALLINT', 'INT', 'BIGINT')`                                | Match all integer widths                                                                                                       |\n| `IS_BIGINT(s)`                 | `schema_of_variant(v) = 'BIGINT'`                                                                 |                                                                                                                                |\n| `IS_SMALLINT(s)`               | `schema_of_variant(v) = 'SMALLINT'`                                                               |                                                                                                                                |\n| `IS_DECIMAL(s)`                | `STARTS_WITH(schema_of_variant(v), 'DECIMAL')`                                                    | `DECIMAL(p,s)` is parameterized                                                                                                |\n| `IS_FLOAT(s)`                  | `schema_of_variant(v) IN ('FLOAT', 'DOUBLE')`                                                     | DBSQL VARIANT collapses fractional types                                                                                       |\n| `IS_BOOLEAN(s)`                | `schema_of_variant(v) = 'BOOLEAN'`                                                                |                                                                                                                                |\n| `IS_VARCHAR(s)` / `IS_CHAR(s)` | `schema_of_variant(v) = 'STRING'`                                                                 | DBSQL has one string type                                                                                                      |\n| `IS_SCALAR(s)`                 | `NOT (STARTS_WITH(schema_of_variant(v), 'ARRAY') OR STARTS_WITH(schema_of_variant(v), 'OBJECT'))` | True for any non-collection type                                                                                               |\n| `JSON_TYPEOF(s)`               | `LOWER(REGEXP_EXTRACT(schema_of_variant(v), '^[A-Z]+', 0))`                                       | Strips parameters/element types and lower-cases to match Redshift's lowercase output (`'bigint'`, `'array'`, `'object'`, etc.) |\n\n> For full VARIANT semantics and `:` path syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/variant-type.md`.\n\n### DML patterns\n\nRedshift supports PostgreSQL-style `UPDATE ... FROM` and `DELETE ... USING`; DBSQL does **not** support either form. Rewrite as correlated subquery or `MERGE`.\n\n| Redshift                            | DBSQL                                                          | Notes                                                         |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `INSERT INTO ... SELECT ...`        | `INSERT INTO ... SELECT ...`                                   | Same                                                          |\n| `INSERT INTO t (cols) VALUES (...)` | `INSERT INTO t (cols) VALUES (...)`                            | Same; multi-row `VALUES` supported                            |\n| `UPDATE t SET ... FROM s WHERE ...` | `MERGE INTO t USING s ... WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET ...`    | DBSQL UPDATE has no FROM clause — see example below           |\n| `DELETE FROM t USING s WHERE ...`   | `DELETE FROM t WHERE <col> IN (SELECT <col> FROM s WHERE ...)` | DBSQL DELETE has no USING clause                              |\n| `TRUNCATE TABLE t` / `TRUNCATE t`   | `TRUNCATE TABLE t`                                             | `TRUNCATE` without `TABLE` is Redshift-only — add the keyword |\n\n**UPDATE ... FROM — example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nUPDATE orders\nSET status = s.new_status\nFROM order_updates s\nWHERE orders.order_id = s.order_id;\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: correlated subquery\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders\nSET status = (\n  SELECT new_status FROM my_catalog.my_schema.order_updates s\n  WHERE s.order_id = orders.order_id\n)\nWHERE order_id IN (SELECT order_id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.order_updates);\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: MERGE (preferred for multi-column updates)\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders AS t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.order_updates AS s ON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET status = s.new_status;\n```\n\n**DELETE ... USING — example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nDELETE FROM stale_orders USING recent_orders r\nWHERE stale_orders.order_id = r.order_id;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDELETE FROM my_catalog.my_schema.stale_orders\nWHERE order_id IN (SELECT order_id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.recent_orders);\n```\n\n### MERGE — Redshift predicate forms\n\nRedshift supports both the **full** form (`WHEN MATCHED ... WHEN NOT MATCHED ...`) and a compact **`REMOVE DUPLICATES`** form. Full syntax (per AWS docs):\n\n```\nMERGE INTO target_table\n  USING source_table [ [ AS ] alias ] ON match_condition\n  [ WHEN MATCHED THEN { UPDATE SET col = expr [,...] | DELETE }\n    WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT [ (cols) ] VALUES (expr [,...])\n  | REMOVE DUPLICATES ]\n```\n\n`REMOVE DUPLICATES` requires source and target to have the **same number of columns, compatible types, and the same column order**. It both updates matched rows and inserts unmatched ones — semantically an upsert. The distinguishing feature: when multiple rows in the target match the same source row, the duplicates are collapsed to a single row (kept + updated). Translate to DBSQL's explicit form:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nMERGE INTO target USING source ON target.id = source.id REMOVE DUPLICATES;\n\n-- DBSQL (explicit clauses — semantically equivalent upsert)\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target AS t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.source AS s ON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\n```\n\n**Edge case**: if the target had pre-existing duplicates on the join key, Redshift's `REMOVE DUPLICATES` collapses them to one row during the merge. DBSQL `MERGE` will raise a multi-match error in that scenario (you can't have multiple target rows match one source row). If the source workload relied on the dedup behavior, pre-deduplicate the target (e.g. `DELETE` stale duplicates) before the `MERGE`, or use a window-function-based rewrite.\n\n**WITH-clause INSERT**: Redshift `INSERT INTO t WITH cte AS (...) SELECT * FROM cte` — DBSQL accepts this form unchanged.\n\n### Aggregate and analytic function drift\n\n| Redshift                                                                   | DBSQL                                   | Notes                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `APPROXIMATE COUNT(DISTINCT col)`                                          | `APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(col)`            | Syntactic, not behavioral                                                   |\n| `MEDIAN(col)`                                                              | `MEDIAN(col)` or `PERCENTILE(col, 0.5)` | `MEDIAN` is supported in DBSQL                                              |\n| `PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col)` (and `PERCENTILE_DISC`) | Identical syntax in DBSQL               | DBSQL accepts the same `WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col)` form — leave unchanged |\n| `STDDEV_SAMP`, `STDDEV_POP`, `VAR_SAMP`, `VAR_POP`                         | Same names                              | Identical                                                                   |\n| `REGR_SLOPE`, `REGR_INTERCEPT`, `REGR_R2`                                  | Same names                              | Identical                                                                   |\n| `LAG(col, n, default) IGNORE NULLS`                                        | `LAG(col, n, default) IGNORE NULLS`     | `IGNORE NULLS` is supported in DBSQL                                        |\n| `RATIO_TO_REPORT(col)`                                                     | `col / SUM(col) OVER ()`                | No direct equivalent; rewrite                                               |\n| `BOOL_AND(col)`                                                            | `BOOL_AND(col)`                         | Identical (synonym for `EVERY`)                                             |\n| `BOOL_OR(col)`                                                             | `BOOL_OR(col)`                          | Identical (synonym for `SOME`/`ANY`)                                        |\n| `ARRAY_AGG(col [ORDER BY ...])`                                            | `COLLECT_LIST(col)` or `ARRAY_AGG(col)` | DBSQL has both; ordered aggregation requires window function                |\n| `ANY_VALUE(col)`                                                           | `ANY_VALUE(col)`                        | Native in DBSQL (DBR 11.3+); non-deterministic in both dialects             |\n\n### Window functions and frame clauses\n\n`ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`, `DENSE_RANK`, `NTILE`, `PERCENT_RANK`, `CUME_DIST`, `LAG`, `LEAD`, `FIRST_VALUE`, `LAST_VALUE`, `NTH_VALUE` — **all native and identical in DBSQL**. `IGNORE NULLS` works on `LAG`/`LEAD`/`FIRST_VALUE`/`LAST_VALUE`. Window frame clauses (`ROWS BETWEEN ... AND ...`, `RANGE BETWEEN ... AND ...`, `UNBOUNDED PRECEDING`, `CURRENT ROW`, `n PRECEDING`, `n FOLLOWING`) carry through unchanged.\n\nAny aggregate above (`SUM`, `MEDIAN`, `STDDEV_*`, `VAR_*`, `LISTAGG`, `PERCENTILE_CONT`/`DISC`, etc.) can be used as a window function via `OVER (...)` — semantics carry through unchanged from the aggregate form.\n\n### Regex and string-parsing drift\n\n| Redshift                                    | DBSQL                                                                                                | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `REGEXP_SUBSTR(str, pat [, pos [, occur]])` | `REGEXP_EXTRACT(str, pat, 0)`                                                                        | DBSQL `REGEXP_EXTRACT`'s 3rd arg is the **capture group index** (0 = whole match), not occurrence. For `occur > 1`, use `REGEXP_EXTRACT_ALL` and index                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `REGEXP_COUNT(str, pat)`                    | `REGEXP_COUNT(str, pat)`                                                                             | Native in DBSQL — identical signature                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `REGEXP_REPLACE(str, pat, repl)`            | `REGEXP_REPLACE(str, pat, repl)`                                                                     | Identical                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| `REGEXP_INSTR(str, pat)`                    | `CASE WHEN REGEXP_EXTRACT(str, pat, 0) = '' THEN 0 ELSE INSTR(str, REGEXP_EXTRACT(str, pat, 0)) END` | Extract the first match, then find its 1-based position. **The `CASE` guard is required** — without it, `REGEXP_EXTRACT` returns `''` on no-match and `INSTR(str, '')` returns `1`, silently flipping Redshift's \"not found → 0\" contract. **Caveat**: `INSTR` returns the position of the first literal occurrence of the extracted text, which can differ from the regex match position for anchored patterns (`^`, `$`), word boundaries, or lookarounds — e.g. `REGEXP_INSTR('hello world hello', 'hello$')` is `13`, but the DBSQL rewrite returns `1`. For anchored or complex patterns, emit a TODO or rewrite with a UDF. |\n| `SPLIT_PART(str, sep, n)`                   | `SPLIT_PART(str, sep, n)`                                                                            | Identical; 1-indexed in both                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `SPLIT_TO_ARRAY(str, sep)`                  | `SPLIT(str, sep)`                                                                                    | **Separator is a Java regex in DBSQL, literal in Redshift.** If `sep` contains regex metacharacters (`.`, `\\|`, `[`, `\\`, `+`, `*`, `(`, `)`, `?`, `{`, `}`, `^`, `$`), escape with `\\\\Q...\\\\E`: `SPLIT(str, CONCAT('\\\\Q', sep, '\\\\E'))`. Literal ASCII separators (`,`, `-`, `:`, `/`) need no change.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n### System catalog / metadata queries\n\nRedshift system views (`STV_*`, `STL_*`, `SVV_*`, `SVCS_*`, `SVL_*`, `PG_*`, `PG_CATALOG.*`, `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.*`) have no direct DBSQL equivalents. Migrate to Unity Catalog information schema or `SHOW` commands:\n\n| Redshift                                                                       | DBSQL equivalent                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SELECT * FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES`                                      | `SHOW TABLES IN catalog.schema` or `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.tables`      |\n| `SELECT * FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS WHERE TABLE_NAME = 't'`              | `DESCRIBE TABLE catalog.schema.t` or `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.columns`   |\n| `SELECT * FROM PG_TABLE_DEF`                                                   | `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.columns`                                        |\n| `SELECT * FROM SVV_TABLE_INFO`                                                 | `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.tables`                                         |\n| `SELECT * FROM STL_QUERY` / `STL_LOAD_ERRORS` / `STV_WLM_SERVICE_CLASS_CONFIG` | No equivalent — use Databricks monitoring UI, query history, or `system.access.*` tables |\n| `SELECT pg_get_viewdef(...)`                                                   | `SHOW CREATE TABLE catalog.schema.view` or `DESCRIBE EXTENDED catalog.schema.view`       |\n\nIf the source uses system views to enumerate objects dynamically (e.g., to build dynamic SQL over all tables in a schema), consider whether the pattern can be replaced with a fixed-schema approach or a notebook cell using the Databricks SDK.\n\n### Safe set operators\n\n`UNION`, `UNION ALL`, `INTERSECT`, `EXCEPT` / `MINUS` — identical in both dialects. Redshift `MINUS` is an alias for `EXCEPT`; DBSQL accepts both.\n"}}]);
